  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed strategy leverages a hierarchical optimization approach that combines coarse-to-fine discretization with gradient-based refinement to address the non-convex nature of the problem.\n\nKey innovations:\n- **Coarse-to-fine discretization**: Start optimization at low resolution to quickly escape local minima, then transfer to high resolution for precise refinement.\n- **Surrogate objective with smooth maximum**: Approximate the non-differentiable max overlap with a differentiable function for efficient gradient descent.\n- **Adaptive gradient descent with constraints**: Use the Adam optimizer with adaptive learning rates for fast convergence while maintaining feasibility through projection steps.\n- **Hybrid exploration-exploitation**: Balance global exploration at coarse grids with local exploitation at fine grids.\n\nAddressed bottlenecks:\n- Non-convexity of the objective function.\n- High dimensionality with dense overlap interactions.\n- Need for efficient constraint handling during updates.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points} such that:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- sum_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_{k=0}^{2n_points-2} [ (convolve(h, 1-h, mode='full'))[k] * dx ]\n\nNew elements:\n- Replace max with a smooth approximation: C\u2085 \u2248 (1/\u03b2) * log(sum(exp(\u03b2 * overlap[k])))\n- Use Adam optimizer for gradient updates\n- Apply projection to enforce constraints after each step\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization\n1. If initial_h_values exists, use as starting point.\n2. Else, initialize h as uniform: h[i] = 0.5\n3. Clip to [0,1] and ensure sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nb) Coarse grid optimization\n1. Downsample h to coarse resolution (n = 50)\n2. Apply Adam optimizer for 2000 steps:\n   a. Compute smooth max overlap gradient\n   b. Update h using Adam\n   c. Project to feasible region\n3. Track best coarse solution\n\nc) Transfer to fine grid\n1. Upsample best coarse solution to n_points = 200\n2. Initialize fine grid h with upscaled values\n\nd) Fine grid optimization\n1. Continue with Adam for 2000 steps:\n   a. Compute gradient of smooth max\n   b. Update h\n   c. Project to [0,1] and normalize sum\n2. Track best fine solution\n\ne) Constraint handling\nAfter each update:\n1. Clip h[i] to [0,1]\n2. Normalize sum(h) = n_points / 2 exactly\n\nf) Termination\n1. Stop if time exceeds budget\n2. Early stopping if no improvement in 500 steps\n3. Return best found solution\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Coarse grid size: 50 (balance exploration vs. computation)\n- Fine grid size: 200 (trade-off between precision and cost)\n- Adam parameters: lr=0.01, \u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999\n- Smooth max \u03b2: 10 (controls smoothness of max approximation)\n- Gradient step size: Central finite differences with \u03b5=1e-5\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Coarse optimization: ~200 iterations, 200ms each\n- Transfer: ~50ms\n- Fine optimization: ~2000 steps, 250ms each\n- Total: ~60s\n- Robustness: Early stopping, best solution retention\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h validated through evaluate_erdos_solution\n- Sum constraint checked with tolerance 1e-6\n- Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Return as (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
